{"status": "success", "data": {"classes": [{"strm": 2881, "crseId": 373312, "crseOfferNbr": 3, "subject": "LGBT", "catalogNbr": "3635", "titleShort": "Queer Classics", "titleLong": "Queer Classics", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 11451, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "01:25PM", "timeEnd": "02:40PM", "startDt": "08/26/2024", "endDt": "12/09/2024", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "cjl299", "firstName": "Cat", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Lambert"}], "pattern": "MW", "facilityDescr": "Goldwin Smith Hall 122", "bldgDescr": "Goldwin Smith Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "GSH122", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [{"classNotesSeq": 1, "descrlong": "Please contact Ryan Belle (rtb95) to be added to the wait list."}], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "08/26/2024", "endDt": "12/21/2024", "addConsent": "I", "addConsentDescr": "Instructor Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "CLASS", "catalogNbr": "3635", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "FGSS", "catalogNbr": "3636", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "SHUM", "catalogNbr": "3635", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "08/26/2024", "sessionEndDt": "12/21/2024", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "This course engages classical antiquity and its reception through the prism of queer studies. Cruising Homer, Sappho, Euripides, Plato, Ovid and more, we will explore how queer theoretical frameworks help us account for premodern queer and trans bodies, desires, experiences, and aesthetics. We will trace how people historically have engaged with the classical past in political and affective projects of writing queer history and literature, constructing identities and communities, and imagining queer futures. We will unpack how classical scholarship might reproduce contemporary forms of homophobia and transphobia in its treatments of gender, sexuality, and embodiment in the classical past, and in turn how modern uses of the classical might reinforce or dismantle exclusionary narratives around 'queerness' today as it intersects with race, gender, sexuality, and class. Finally, we will consider how the work we are doing in this class (where the 'Queer' in 'Queer Classics' may be taken as an adjective or an imperative) relates to the ways that contemporary writers, activists, artists, and performers have animated the classical past with queer possibilities. All readings will be in translation; no knowledge of Latin and Greek is required.", "catalogBreadth": null, "catalogDistr": "(ALC-AS, SCD-AS) (CA-AG, D-AG, LA-AG)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2881, "crseId": 359467, "crseOfferNbr": 3, "subject": "LGBT", "catalogNbr": "3740", "titleShort": "Parody", "titleLong": "Parody", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 18201, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "01:25PM", "timeEnd": "02:40PM", "startDt": "08/26/2024", "endDt": "12/09/2024", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "ngs9", "firstName": "Nick", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Salvato"}], "pattern": "MW", "facilityDescr": "Schwartz Ctr-Perform Arts 124", "bldgDescr": "Schwartz Ctr-Performing Arts", "facilityDescrshort": "SWZ124", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "08/26/2024", "endDt": "12/21/2024", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "AMST", "catalogNbr": "3745", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "FGSS", "catalogNbr": "3740", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "PMA", "catalogNbr": "3740", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "08/26/2024", "sessionEndDt": "12/21/2024", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "In A Theory of Parody, Linda Hutcheon defines parody broadly as \"repetition with critical difference, which marks difference rather than similarity.\" Taking a cue from Hutcheon, we will consider parody as a form of meaning making that is not necessarily used in the service of ridicule. Rather, we will examine a number of late-twentieth- and early-twenty-first-century imitative works in order to distinguish the rich variety of political agendas and aesthetic rationales for recent parody. An emphasis on postmodern or contemporary performances and media that renovate images, ideas, and icons from modernism and modernity will unite our otherwise diverse efforts. Some of these efforts will also highlight what happens when an artist takes up a work made for one platform (for example, theatre, performance art, installation, cinema, television, the Web) and parodies it in another. Creators and works under consideration may range from Christopher Durang, Split Britches, and Pig Iron Theatre Company to The Simpsons, Cookie's Fortune, and Strindberg and Helium.", "catalogBreadth": null, "catalogDistr": "(ALC-AS) (CA-AG, LA-AG)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2881, "crseId": 370485, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "LGBT", "catalogNbr": "3990", "titleShort": "Undergrad Independent Study", "titleLong": "Undergraduate Independent Study", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "IND", "ssrComponentLong": "Independent Study", "section": "601", "classNbr": 9631, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "", "timeEnd": "", "startDt": "08/26/2024", "endDt": "12/09/2024", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "slw42", "firstName": "Sara", "middleName": "L.", "lastName": "Warner"}], "pattern": "", "facilityDescr": null, "bldgDescr": null, "facilityDescrshort": null, "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [{"classNotesSeq": 1, "descrlong": "To apply: first discuss the Independent Study with the course supervisor, and then complete the online Independent Study form at https://data.arts.cornell.edu/as-stus/indep_study_intro.cfm."}], "campus": "MAIN", "ca